# Agent skill library layout

A **vendor-agnostic layout** for encoding team knowledge so AI assistants invoke the right procedure at the right time. In this pack: **skills** → `.cursor/skills/<slug>/`, **rules** → `.cursor/rules/<slug>.mdc`, **agents** → `.cursor/agents/<name>.md`, **commands** → `.cursor/commands/`. Workflow topics (`ship`, `concise`, `simplicity`, `delegation`, `skill-library`) live under `rules/`; Cursor subagent presets live under `agents/`.

### Root `AGENTS.md` (required)

Every repo using this layout needs a **root `AGENTS.md`** — the agent's entry point. It is repo-specific and self-maintaining; the transferable patterns stay in this pack's `rules/`.

| File | Scope | Agent updates? |
| ---- | ----- | -------------- |
| **Repo root `AGENTS.md`** | Bootstrap order, repo map, commands, learned facts | Yes — append discoveries |
| **`.cursor/rules/<slug>.mdc`** | Transferable patterns (lint, contract mode, security, …) | Propose via PR only |
| **`.cursor/skills/<slug>/`** | Procedures (`SKILL.md` + companions) | Propose new skills |

Root `AGENTS.md` must include: bootstrap checklist, doc hierarchy, delivery pipeline with skill paths, decision router, **Repo map** (TODO rows agents fill), **Learned facts** (append-only), and **Self-maintenance** rules (when to update which section). Start from [`references/pack/agents-root-template.md`](../references/pack/agents-root-template.md).

Agents read root `AGENTS.md` first every session, then [`../AGENTS.md`](../AGENTS.md) for pack routing — never duplicate a rule's body into either file.

### Directory layout (this pack)

```
.cursor/
  AGENTS.md                 # pack entry: pipelines, indexes, operating rules
  agents/<name>.md          # Cursor subagent presets
  commands/ship.md          # sole command
  rules/<slug>.mdc          # topics + workflow + subagents.mdc (all always-on)
  skills/<slug>/SKILL.md
  references/<cat>/<slug>.md  # deep refs: spec/, ship/, engineering/, data-systems/, system-design-101/, refactoring/, lint/, pack/
```

Register skills in your assistant's skill manifest pointing at `.cursor/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md`.

### Skill taxonomy

| Tier | Purpose | Examples |
| ---- | ------- | -------- |
| **Delivery trunk** | Idea → PRD → tickets → code | grill, spec, to-tickets, build, tdd, code-review |
| **Contract lifecycle** | Durable `SPEC.md` mode | contract, contract-check, contract-backprop |
| **On-ramps** | Enter the trunk from a queue or a bug | triage, wayfinder, diagnosing-bugs, handoff |
| **Design** | Vocabulary before restructuring | codebase-design, clean-ddd-hexagonal, domain-modeling · rule [`folder-structure`](./folder-structure.mdc) |
| **Discipline** | YAGNI + token efficiency | simplicity (+ review/audit/debt companions) |
| **Quality** | Cross-cutting standards | logging-best-practices, design |
| **Landing** | Reviewable PR delivery | stacking-prs (`gh stack`; layer budgets) |
| **Stack** | Framework/tool depth (opt-in per repo) | Per-language folders with `references/` subdocs |

**Rule:** Core and pre-build skills are stack-agnostic. Stack skills are opt-in per repo — install only what you use.

### SKILL.md anatomy

Each skill file declares:

```yaml
---
name: skill-id
description: |
  When to trigger (phrases, conditions). What it does NOT do.
---
```

Body sections: **WHEN**, **STEPS**, **OUTPUT SHAPE**, **BOUNDARIES**, **NON-GOALS**.

### Single-responsibility boundaries

Section ownership detail → [`contract-format`](../references/spec/contract-format.md). Summary:

| Skill | Contract writes | May write code? | May delegate? |
| ----- | --------------- | --------------- | ------------- |
| contract | Sole general editor (NEW / AMEND / §B+§V on bug) | No | No |
| build | §T status only | Yes | No |
| contract-check | None (read-only) | No | No |
| contract-backprop | Via `contract` | Yes (fix + test) | No |
| grill | §G + §C on OK | No | No |
| research | Append §R | No | Optional gather |
| code-review | None (report only) | No | Yes (review axes) |
| delegation | None | Builder only, ≤2 files | Yes |

**Contract lifecycle:** no sub-agents. Delegation / `/ship` are separate Cursor surfaces — they must not bypass section ownership.

Violating boundaries causes drift — the contract says one thing, code another, and no skill owns the fix.

### Stack skill pattern

Framework skills follow progressive disclosure:

1. **SKILL.md** — when to use, guardrails, output contract, link to references
2. **references/** — deep docs loaded only when needed (keeps main context small)
3. **rules/** (optional) — one file per lintable pattern

Entry-point skill maps commands to specialized skills (e.g. "daily workflow" → "migrations" + "functional tests").

### Agent presets (`.cursor/agents/`)

This pack ships **Tier 1** Cursor subagents under [`../agents/`](../agents/). Workflow essays stay rules: `ship`, `concise`, `delegation`, `skill-library`.

Each subagent file uses YAML frontmatter:

```yaml
---
name: investigator
description: When to delegate — be specific; Agent reads this to auto-delegate
model: inherit          # or a model ID, e.g. claude-opus-4-8[effort=high]
readonly: true          # no file edits
is_background: false    # true = non-blocking parallel work
---
```

Delegation rules for the parent agent live in **[`subagents.mdc`](./subagents.mdc)** (`alwaysApply: true`).

| Preset | readonly | Typical use |
| ------ | -------- | ----------- |
| investigator | yes | file:line search |
| builder | no | ≤2 file surgical edit |
| diff-reviewer | yes | diff audit |
| verifier | yes | post-build check |
| ci-investigator | yes | CI log root cause (background) |
| ship-setup / ship-spec / ship-spec-review / ship-execute / ship-review / ship-verify | varies | `/ship` phases |
| design-architect / design-auditor | yes | Phase 6 design · Phase 8 design audit |

Stack specialists (Symfony, Doctrine, …) are optional at the consuming repo — not in this pack.

Built-in Cursor subagents (`explore`, `bash`, `browser`) need no config.

